1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,420
At 8:05 pm on October 29th,
money starts pouring out of Binance

2
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:10,020
across 10 blockchains at once.

3
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:15,300
20 minutes later, $6.3 billion is gone.

4
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:20,540
Users open the app to pull their cash out
and the withdraw button is grayed out.

5
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:23,580
When Binance pulls the logs,
nothing adds up.

6
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:27,820
No blockchain got broken,
not a single private key was leaked.

7
00:00:27,820 --> 00:00:29,820
Every transfer that drained those wallets

8
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:34,100
was legitimately signed
by Binance's own internal system.

9
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:38,780
Whoever pulled this off had been
inside the company for five months.

10
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:42,580
And the way they got in had
nothing to do with breaking code.

11
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:44,340
Rewind to June.

12
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:47,460
An engineer on Binance's
wallet team is at his desk,

13
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:50,620
searching Google
for a popular AI coding tool.

14
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:53,020
He finds a download link,
runs the installer,

15
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:55,660
and gets back to work, routine stuff.

16
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:58,660
Except the installer is tampered with.

17
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:01,540
Now most companies would
already be toast right here.

18
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:04,300
Except Binance actually catches it.

19
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:06,540
Their security team
spots the weird activity,

20
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:11,100
pulls the engineer's laptop off the desk,
wipes access, rotates every credential,

21
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,980
and closes the ticket.
On paper, that's a win.

22
00:01:13,980 --> 00:01:15,540
They stopped it cold.

23
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:17,100
Or so they thought.

24
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:19,340
What they didn't realize
was that before they

25
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:22,140
yanked that laptop,
the attackers had already grabbed

26
00:01:22,140 --> 00:01:25,220
the one thing you can't
reset with a password change,

27
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:28,220
a map of who works on the wallet team.

28
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:30,260
They didn't need that laptop anymore.

29
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:33,220
They had names. So they went quiet.

30
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:36,460
For three whole months, nothing happened.

31
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:39,700
Then in September, a second
software engineer at Binance

32
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:41,860
gets a message on a job site.

33
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:45,700
It's from someone claiming to be
a project director at a tech startup.

34
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,820
The engineer has Binance
right there on his resume

35
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:49,900
and the offer sounds legitimate.

36
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:52,540
This wasn't some sloppy spam email.

37
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:56,740
The guy gets scheduled for a real round
one video interview with an HR rep.

38
00:01:56,740 --> 00:01:58,740
During the call, the rep even shares

39
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:01,500
a social media profile
filled with personal photos

40
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:04,540
and videos just to look
like a real human being.

41
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:06,140
He passes round one.

42
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:10,260
Then he does a round two interview
with the supposed CEO of this company,

43
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:12,780
a company that didn't even exist.

44
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:15,900
At the end, they send him
the standard coding take-home test.

45
00:02:15,900 --> 00:02:19,860
He downloads it onto his work
laptop and runs the setup script.

46
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:24,180
And inside that script,
a tiny piece of code goes to work.

47
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:27,420
It finds a normal developer
tool buried in his system,

48
00:02:27,420 --> 00:02:31,740
a file that's exactly 3,344 bytes.

49
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:33,900
It deletes it and drops in a lookalike

50
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:39,660
that's 3,414 bytes,
70 bytes of difference.

51
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:42,300
Binance's endpoint security didn't blink.

52
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:44,620
No pop-up, no red flag.

53
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:50,180
And the engineer probably went on with his
week thinking he just nailed an interview.

54
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:53,020
Now, you might be thinking,
why go through all that trouble

55
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:56,300
for a guy who doesn't even
hold the keys to the vault?

56
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,660
Because he doesn't need to.

57
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:00,380
He just needs to be inside the building.

58
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:04,820
On October 20th, sitting silently
inside that second engineer's laptop,

59
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:07,380
the attackers don't
look for crypto wallets.

60
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:09,300
They look for a bridge.

61
00:03:09,300 --> 00:03:15,900
And they find one, right inside Binance's
own office software, an internal AI tool.

62
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:20,940
Using that AI tool as a springboard, they
jump sideways onto two other computers.

63
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:23,660
One belongs to another
wallet software engineer,

64
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,740
and the other belongs
to the exact same guy from June,

65
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:30,540
the one whose laptop Binance thought
they'd cleaned up months earlier.

66
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:34,020
And sitting right there on his
machine is a deployment token.

67
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:35,820
Basically, a digital badge that lets you

68
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:39,020
push new software
on the company infrastructure.

69
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:41,020
Still, nothing moves.

70
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:43,500
They wait another eight days.

71
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:45,780
On October 28th, a brand new wallet

72
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:49,420
quietly pulls three tiny
withdrawals out of a crypto mixer.

73
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,340
Just 0.1 Ether at a time
to pay for transaction fees.

74
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:58,460
36 hours later,
on October 29th at 5:15 pm UTC,

75
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:01,780
the attackers log on to that
third engineer's computer,

76
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,020
grab the deployment token.

77
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:05,900
They lift it from the first guy,
launch a container,

78
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:10,060
and walk straight into the system
that approves withdrawals.

79
00:04:10,060 --> 00:04:13,340
Here's the part that sounds
crazy until you picture it.

80
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:15,900
They didn't steal the vault's master keys.

81
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:19,140
They walked up to the automated
teller inside the vault

82
00:04:19,140 --> 00:04:23,420
and got it to pre-sign 50 blank
permission slips on Ethereum.

83
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:24,700
Why 50?

84
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,060
Because every account
has a running counter.

85
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:29,060
And they just needed one of those 50 slips

86
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:32,100
to land on the exact number
waiting in the queue.

87
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:35,140
At 8:05 pm, they flipped the switch.

88
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,780
Funds start leaving Binance
across 10 blockchains at once.

89
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:43,060
XRP, Bitcoin, Tron, Solana, Ethereum.

90
00:04:43,060 --> 00:04:45,820
20 minutes past the hour,
one of those 50 slips

91
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:48,780
catches on Ethereum,
turning a main Binance wallet

92
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:51,140
into a self-emptying machine.

93
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:53,500
Three minutes and 24 seconds later,

94
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:58,740
over 167,000 Ether
and $1.6 billion in Tether

95
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:01,540
disappear in a single sweep.

96
00:05:01,540 --> 00:05:04,940
People watching online that night
couldn't wrap their heads around it.

97
00:05:04,940 --> 00:05:09,540
If you try to pull $5,000 out of a regular
bank, a manager calls your phone.

98
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:14,020
Somehow $6.3 billion just
walked out in 20 minutes

99
00:05:14,020 --> 00:05:17,180
without a single human
stepping in front of the door.

100
00:05:17,180 --> 00:05:19,900
And then, the real race started.

101
00:05:19,900 --> 00:05:22,620
Binance froze withdrawals
and hit the phones.

102
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:26,060
Within hours, Tether,
Circle, Ripple, and Arbitrum

103
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:28,300
slammed the emergency
brakes on their networks.

104
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:31,980
Blacklisting wallets and locking
up roughly $2.7 billion

105
00:05:31,980 --> 00:05:34,340
in stablecoins before they could move.

106
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:37,820
Except the attackers weren't sitting
back watching the scoreboard.

107
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:39,620
They knew the freeze buttons were coming.

108
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:43,940
So they started dumping at-risk Tether
to market makers as fast as they could.

109
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:46,820
They even shoved $60
million of stolen Tether

110
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:49,940
into a lending protocol just
to borrow clean Ether against it.

111
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:52,740
By midnight,
they pulled off one final wave

112
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:55,620
on Avalanche and Polygon
before Binance finally

113
00:05:55,620 --> 00:05:59,020
cut the access off at 12:10 AM.

114
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:02,500
When the smoke cleared,
nearly $3 billion was frozen.

115
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:03,980
But the hackers were still holding

116
00:06:03,980 --> 00:06:09,260
over 420,000 Ether and 18,200 Bitcoin.

117
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:14,540
Roughly $2.4 billion in raw
assets that no CEO, no bank,

118
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:18,020
and no government can freeze
with the click of a button.

119
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:21,260
Two days later, Binance
turned withdrawals back on,

120
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:26,060
processed $4.6 billion of customer
cash-outs in a single day.

121
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:31,580
And by November 12, announced it had
plugged the entire $6.3 billion hole out

122
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:34,820
of its own emergency funds
and corporate treasury.

123
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:37,460
That same morning, the FBI
and the Justice Department

124
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:41,620
got a court order to seize
1.2 billion of the frozen pile

125
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:43,900
and publicly pinned the whole operation

126
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:48,460
on North Korea's TraderTraitor
unit, almost four times bigger

127
00:06:48,460 --> 00:06:51,220
than the Bybit heist just a year earlier.

128
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:57,660
So users got paid back,
withdrawals work, case closed, right?

129
00:06:57,660 --> 00:06:58,820
Not quite.

130
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:02,420
Because if you look closely at what
happened after the headlines faded,

131
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:05,740
there's an elephant sitting
right in the middle of the room.

132
00:07:05,740 --> 00:07:11,220
On October 30, Binance promised deeper
technical answers within 48 hours.

133
00:07:11,220 --> 00:07:15,140
Weeks later, that full technical
breakdown still hasn't dropped.

134
00:07:15,140 --> 00:07:18,620
To this day, Binance still hasn't
said which internal AI tool

135
00:07:18,620 --> 00:07:22,260
let an attacker bounce across
three engineers' laptops.

136
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:25,340
They still haven't explained how
a single compromised container

137
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:29,020
got an internal signing system
to approve 50 blank checks

138
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:32,380
with zero rate limits or human sign-off.

139
00:07:32,380 --> 00:07:35,260
And buried inside Binance's own report

140
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:40,100
was one line that should make anyone
working in tech pause for a second.

141
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:42,900
They admitted that in 2026 alone,

142
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:46,500
they caught 57 other attempts
just like this one.

143
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:52,100
Fake interviews, poison tools, malicious
downloads, targeting their engineers.

144
00:07:52,100 --> 00:07:54,060
They stopped 57 of them.

145
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:58,300
It only took one 70-byte
file to make it through.
